Fix Ghidra's Intel 8051 language for emulation.

Ghidra's 8051 specification targets decompilation and gets these wrong when
emulated; nix/ghidra.nix recompiles 8051.sla with this patch applied:

- ADDC and SUBB fold the carry into the operand in one byte, so an operand of
  0xFF with the carry set wraps to 0 and the carry or borrow out is lost.
- ADD, ADDC and SUBB never set AC or OV, so DA A misadjusts and signed
  overflow goes undetected.
- Arithmetic and logic instructions write invented N and Z flags into PSW bits
  5 and 1, which are the user flags F0 and F1.
- Bit instructions use a separate byte per bit (BIT_OPS "BIT_ADDRS") instead of
  the bits of internal RAM 0x20-0x2F and of the SFRs.
- SP has no direct-address alias, so `mov sp,#n` writes SFR 0x81 and leaves SP
  alone.  With the alias, PUSH and POP of SP would use SP as both operand and
  stack pointer, so the patch passes their operand through a temporary.
- MOVX @Ri ignores P2, which supplies the high address byte, so code that
  pages external RAM, as SDCC's startup code does, stays in its first 256
  bytes.  The patch takes the page from a new register, XPAGE, which the host
  keeps in step with the SFR another, XPAGE_SFR, names, since Silicon Labs'
  CIP-51 devices page through an EMI0CN instead.
- SFRs have no pages, so Silicon Labs' devices that page their SFRs through
  SFRPAGE cannot be emulated.  The patch moves SFR accesses to a new space,
  PSFR, whose addresses have a second byte, the page, as the CIP-51 variant's
  SFR space has; a new register, SFR_PAGE, which the host keeps in step with
  SFRPAGE, selects the page of the SFRs that two more, SFR_PAGED_LO and
  SFR_PAGED_HI, mark as paged.

--- a/Ghidra/Processors/8051/data/languages/8051_main.sinc
+++ b/Ghidra/Processors/8051/data/languages/8051_main.sinc
@@ -1,8 +1,11 @@
 # sleigh specification file for Intel 8051
 
 #@define BIT_OPS "PCODEOPS"
-#@define BIT_OPS "SHIFTS" 
-@define BIT_OPS "BIT_ADDRS"
+# SmallWorld: SHIFTS, so bit instructions read and write the bits of internal
+# RAM 0x20-0x2F and of the SFRs, as the hardware does, rather than a separate
+# byte per bit.
+@define BIT_OPS "SHIFTS"
+#@define BIT_OPS "BIT_ADDRS"
 
 # It's sometimes clearer for decompilation to omit the pushing and
 # restoring of the return value for function calls.
@@ -201,6 +204,25 @@
 define register offset=0x40  size=$(SP_SIZE) [ SP ];
 define register offset=0x44  size=$(PTRSIZE) [ PC ];
 define register offset=0x48  size=1 [ PSW ];
+@if defined(MCS51)
+# SmallWorld: the address of the SFR that supplies the high byte of MOVX @Ri's
+# address.  Not a register of the hardware but a property of the device: P2
+# (0xA0) on Intel's 8051, an EMI0CN on Silicon Labs' CIP-51 devices.  The host
+# sets it before emulation starts, and keeps XPAGE in step with that SFR - or
+# zero, where it is not mapped - so that MOVX @Ri reads no SFR itself.
+define register offset=0x4C  size=1 [ XPAGE_SFR ];
+define register offset=0x4E  size=1 [ XPAGE ];
+# SmallWorld: SFR paging, as on Silicon Labs' devices with an SFRPAGE SFR.
+# SFRs are emulated in PSFR, whose addresses have a second byte, the page, as
+# the CIP-51 variant's SFR space has; unpaged SFRs are on page 0.  None of
+# these registers is the hardware's.  SFR_PAGE mirrors SFRPAGE, which the host
+# keeps it in step with, since SFRPAGE's address depends on the device.  Bit n
+# of SFR_PAGED_LO says whether SFR 0x80 + n is paged; of SFR_PAGED_HI, whether
+# SFR 0xC0 + n is.  The host leaves both zero on other devices.
+define space PSFR     type=ram_space      size=2;
+define register offset=0x4D  size=1 [ SFR_PAGE ];
+define register offset=0x58  size=8 [ SFR_PAGED_LO SFR_PAGED_HI ];
+@endif
